course: everyday
lesson: 223
title: Queues, taking turns and interrupting
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A queue is fair because the people at the front have waited longest, so you join the end, keep your hands to yourself and wait patiently.
explain: In the UK people value queuing very highly, and jumping the queue makes them upset; knowing such customs helps you avoid offending anyone.
explain: Taking turns ends arguments fairly: on the swings, in the front seat of the car, and in a group discussion, where everyone's ideas matter.
explain: When you need to speak while someone is talking, you wait for a pause, or say "Excuse me" first; you also let people finish their own sentences.
explain: A girl in Melbourne wants to tell her dad something while he is talking to a neighbour; she puts her hand gently on his arm and waits, and he turns to her at the next pause.
fact: You join the end of a queue and wait your turn, because the people at the front waited longest.
fact: In a queue you keep your hands to yourself and do not push, because pushing can make people fall.
fact: You might let an older or disabled person, or a parent with a baby, go ahead of you.
fact: When a queue is slow, you wait patiently and do not complain loudly.
fact: In the UK people value queuing highly and get upset about queue-jumping.
fact: On a swing when others are waiting, you have a turn and then let the next child go.
fact: Brothers and sisters can share the front seat by taking turns, for example on alternate days.
fact: In a group discussion you let others speak and wait for your turn.
fact: To speak while a parent talks to someone, you wait for a pause or put your hand gently on their arm and wait.
fact: You let people finish their own sentences, especially someone who stammers (gets stuck on sounds or repeats them while speaking).
fact: If you must interrupt, you say "Excuse me" or "Sorry to interrupt" first; when it is urgent, like someone being hurt, you interrupt straight away.
fact: When others are watching TV or a film, you wait for a break or talk quietly.
exercise: There is a line of people at the ticket office. What do you do? | Join the end and wait your turn | Walk to the front if you are in a hurry | Stand right next to the front person | Push in wherever there is a little gap
exercise: Why is it fair to join the end of a queue? | The people at the front waited longest | The end of the queue is closer to the door | The front is only for adults | Queues move faster from the back
exercise: You are in the lunch queue and the boy in front is slow. What do you do? | Keep your hands to yourself and wait | Give him a little push to help him along | Squeeze past him | Lean on his back
exercise: An old man with a walking stick is struggling in the long queue behind you. What might you do? | Let him go ahead of you | Pretend not to see him there | Tell him to wait like everyone else | Move away so he can't see you
exercise: The supermarket queue is taking ages. What is polite? | Wait patiently without complaining loudly | Sigh and groan loudly so that the cashier hurries up | Tell everyone the shop is useless | Leave your trolley in the queue
exercise: In London, someone walks to the front of a bus queue. How will people probably feel? | Upset, as the British value queuing highly | Pleased that he is quick | They will not notice | They will all follow him
exercise: You have been on the only swing for a while and two children are waiting. What do you do? | Let the next child have a turn | Keep swinging until it is home time | Swing higher so that they give up | Tell them to go and find another park
exercise: You and your sister both want the front seat every day. What is a fair answer? | Take turns, for example on alternate days | The oldest one always sits in front, every day | Whoever runs fastest gets it | Whoever shouts loudest gets it
exercise: Your group is planning a project and you have lots of ideas. What do you do? | Share yours and let others speak too | Talk the whole time so nothing is missed | Ignore the quiet members | Speak over anyone who disagrees
exercise: Your mum is talking to a neighbour and you want to tell her something. What do you do? | Wait for a pause, or touch her arm gently and wait | Pull her sleeve and talk at once, so she knows it matters | Shout "Mum!" again and again | Tell the neighbour to stop talking
exercise: A classmate who stammers is speaking slowly. What should you do? | Let him finish his own sentence | Finish the sentence for him | Tell him to hurry up | Start talking about something else
exercise: You really must interrupt the teacher's talk with a visitor. How do you start? | "Excuse me" or "Sorry to interrupt" | "Listen to me, this is really important!" | Tap the visitor firmly on the shoulder | Just start talking straight away, loudly
exercise: Your family is watching a film and you want to talk. What do you do? | Wait for a break, or talk quietly | Talk loudly over the film so they hear | Pause it without asking | Stand in front of the TV
exercise: Why can finishing other people's sentences be unkind? | It can feel pushy, especially for someone who stammers | It helps them speak faster | It is a sign of good listening | It shows you are clever
test: A girl in Dallas arrives at a busy ice-cream van. Where should she go? | To the end of the line | Straight over to the window | Next to her friend near the front | In front of the smallest child
test: Children are pushing in the queue for the slide. What could happen? | Someone could fall and get hurt | The slide will go a lot faster for all | Everyone will get more turns that way | Nothing, as pushing is part of play
test: A mum with a crying baby is behind you at the chemist's. What is a kind choice? | Offer to let her go ahead | Tell her the baby is too loud | Ignore her completely and carry on | Leave the queue in annoyance
test: Why do people say queuing is very important in Britain? | British people value fair queuing highly | Queuing is the law in all British shops and cafés | British shops are very small | Only tourists queue there
test: Two brothers argue every morning over who feeds the dog. What is a fair way out? | Take turns on different days | The faster one always does it | Their parents feed the dog instead | The dog decides
test: In a class discussion, Tom always speaks first and longest. What would be fairer? | Letting others speak and waiting his turn | Speaking even louder so that everyone can hear him | Answering for the whole class | Staying silent for ever
test: Which is the polite way to interrupt two grown-ups talking? | "Sorry to interrupt, but..." | Talking over them until they stop | Clapping your hands at them loudly | Pulling at their clothes and coats
test: Grandpa is watching the news and you want to tell him about school. What is best? | Wait for an advert break or talk quietly | Turn the TV off so he can listen properly | Talk louder than the TV | Stand between him and the screen
test: Lucy's dad feels her hand on his arm while he talks. What does it tell him? | She is waiting to say something | She wants him to go home right now, this minute | She is upset with him | She wants to hold hands
test: queue for the ice cream van is massive, gonna sneak in the front | Join the end and wait your turn | Push to the front | Ask your friend to save a place | Pretend you were there first
test: mum's talkin to a lady and i need to tell her something NOW | Wait for a pause or put your hand gently on her arm | Shout over them | Pull on her coat | Stand in front and talk
test: my friend stammers, can i finish his sentences for him to help | No, let him finish his own sentences | Yes, it saves time and he'll be glad you did | Yes, guess what he means | Yes, if you're in a hurry
test: been on the swing ages and theres kids waiting | Have your turn, then let the next child go | Stay until you're bored | Go higher so they leave | Tell them it's yours
explain: A boy in Leeds pushed ahead in the lunch queue and shoved a girl holding hot soup; it spilled on her arm and she was burned.
explain: Children pushing to be first off a school bus in Texas knocked a small boy down the steps; he broke his wrist.
fact: Pushing in queues causes falls, spills and burns, especially near stairs, doors and hot food.
fact: Waiting your turn on stairs and bus steps keeps the smallest children from being knocked down.
test: lunch line so slow, gonna push to the front | Wait your turn, pushing can spill hot food on someone | Push, you're the hungriest | Push gently, then it's fine | Push in where the little kids are
test: Why is shoving on bus steps dangerous? | People can fall down the steps and get hurt | It makes the bus late | The driver loses money | Steps are only for adults
test: mum's on the phone, i need her NOW, sis cut her hand bad | Interrupt straight away, an injury is urgent | Wait till she's done | Write her a note | Text her instead
test: did u hear a kid pushed in the lunch queue and a girl got shoved into the hot soup and burnt her arm? real lesson? | Don't push in queues; pushing near hot food causes burns | Only push when the queue is slow | Lunch queues are always safe | It's the soup's fault for being hot
test: apparently in England people go to prison for queue-jumping, my cousin says. probably true? | No, it's not a crime, but people get upset by it | Yes, it's a serious crime there | Yes, but only in London | Yes, if you jump twice
test: everyone's saying that boy with the stammer takes forever to talk so don't pick him for the quiz team. pass it on? | No, wait patiently and let him finish his words | Yes, the quiz team needs fast talkers to win anything | Yes, but say it nicely | Yes, it's just being practical
exam: Identify the principal reason for which persons join the end of a queue. | Those at the front have waited longest | The end of a queue invariably moves more quickly | It is required only in shops | The front is reserved for adults
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate manner of speaking while a parent converses with another person? | To await a pause or place a hand gently on his arm and wait | To speak loudly until noticed | To pull at his clothing repeatedly | To interrupt at once with one's news
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of a person who stammers. | He is permitted to complete his own sentences | His sentences are completed for him, to spare him effort | He is asked to speak more quickly | He is not asked to speak at all
exam: Ascertain the persons whom one might allow to go ahead in a queue. | Older or disabled persons, or a parent with a baby | Only those persons who request it loudly and repeatedly | Only one's own friends | Only persons who push forward
